<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/2008">2008</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alexander Solzhenitsyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn was a Russian novelist and historian who spent eight years in Soviet labour camps after criticising Stalin in a private letter. His story One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich (1962) was the first published Soviet account of the camps, and The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> Archipelago, published abroad from 1973, documented the whole system. He won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature in 1970, was expelled in 1974 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aleksandr-solzhenitsyn.jpg" alt="Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bert Verhoeff for Anefo · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleksandr_Solzhenitsyn_1974crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Aleksandr Isayevich Solzhenitsyn was born in Kislovodsk on 11 December 1918. His father, Isaakiy Semyonovich Solzhenitsyn, was of Russian descent, and his mother, Taisiya Zakharovna, of Ukrainian descent. The family defied the Soviet anti-religious campaign of the 1920s and remained devout members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>, but at a young age he became an atheist and embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marxism-leninism">Marxism–Leninism</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he commanded a sound-ranging battery in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, was involved in major action at the front, and was twice decorated; he received the Order of the Red Star on 8 July 1944.</p><p>In February 1945, while serving in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-prussia">East Prussia</a>, he was arrested by SMERSH over nineteen months of correspondence with a friend, Nikolai Vitkevich, in which they criticised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> and the conduct of the war.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a> and internal exile, Solzhenitsyn was freed and exonerated following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikita-khrushchev">Nikita Khrushchev</a>'s Secret Speech of 1956. He taught at a secondary school by day and wrote in secret by night. In his Nobel lecture he recalled that until 1961 he was convinced he would never see a line of his work in print. His first novel, One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich, appeared in 1962 with Khrushchev's approval, and Matryona's Place in 1963 was his last work published in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>.</p><p>After Khrushchev's removal in 1964 publication stopped and he became a non-person; by 1965 the KGB had seized some of his papers, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of In the First Circle. Cancer Ward appeared abroad in 1966, In the First Circle in 1968 and August 1914 in 1971.</p><p>He kept working in secret on The Gulag Archipelago, published in 1973. On 12 February 1974 he was arrested and deported the next day to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a>, and stripped of his Soviet citizenship; the KGB had found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of its first part. His citizenship was restored in 1990, and in 1994 he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, living in a dacha at Troitse-Lykovo in west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Solzhenitsyn was awarded the 1970 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature for the ethical force with which he pursued the indispensable traditions of Russian literature. The Gulag Archipelago amounted to a head-on challenge to the Soviet state and sold tens of millions of copies. In later political writings such as Rebuilding Russia he opposed any nostalgia for Soviet communism and called for a strong presidential republic balanced by local self-government. He died of heart failure near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 3 August 2008, aged 89, and was buried at the Donskoy Monastery in a spot he had chosen. A bronze statue of him stands on the seafront in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladivostok">Vladivostok</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Soviet culture combined official socialist realism with world-class achievement in music, ballet, film and literature, from Sergei Prokofiev, Dmitri Shostakovich and Sergei Eisenstein to Boris Pasternak and <b>Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn</b>, whose account of the Gulag shaped Western views of the system.</blockquote><a href="/empire/soviet-union">Soviet Union · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kislovodsk, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.91,42.72&amp;year=1976" aria-label="Kislovodsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5575 608 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Aleksandr Solzhenitsyn"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6025 878h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6025 878h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.91,42.72&amp;year=1976">What was here in 1976? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
